SO-101 RealSense Master Folder & LeRobot Hotfixes
This repository contains the configuration and scripts for the SO-101 RealSense robotics project. It includes a hotfixes/ directory containing heavily modified framework files required to run LeRobot and PyTorch natively on Windows without C++ binary crashes.

The Problem
Running LeRobot and LeLab on Windows with CUDA 12.6 triggers fatal system-level crashes during data loading and video decoding. Specifically:

torchcodec fails to load FFmpeg C++ binaries, resulting in WinError 127: The specified procedure could not be found.

PyTorch background dataloader workers spawn without the main memory context, instantly poisoning the environment and crashing the pipeline.

The Solution (Hotfixes)
To bypass these issues, three core framework files in the .venv were permanently modified. If rebuilding this environment from scratch, you must overwrite the default pip-installed files with the backups located in the hotfixes/ folder.

1. PyAV Video Decoder Override

Target: .venv\Lib\site-packages\lerobot\datasets\video_utils.py

Modification: The default torchcodec logic was completely removed from the decode_video_frames function. The function is now hardcoded to strictly return decode_video_frames_pyav(), forcing the system to bypass the broken DLLs and use PyAV's stable C-libraries for all video decoding.

2. PyTorch Multiprocessing Lobotomy

Target: .venv\Lib\site-packages\torch\utils\data\dataloader.py

Modification: To prevent background workers from crashing the video decoder, PyTorch's native multiprocessing was forcefully disabled. The master overrides num_workers = 0, prefetch_factor = None, and persistent_workers = False were injected at the very top of the __init__ function to intercept and rewrite LeLab's incoming data requests.

3. Checkpoint Saving Fix

Target: .venv\Lib\site-packages\lerobot\common\train_utils.py

Modification: Applied a custom hotfix to the update_last_checkpoint function to ensure training state is correctly written and maintained during the overridden training loops.

Setup Instructions
Clone this repository.

Initialize a virtual environment (.venv) and run the standard pip install for LeRobot and its dependencies.

Copy the three Python files from the hotfixes/ folder and overwrite the default files generated inside your .venv/Lib/site-packages/ directory.

Launch LeLab and begin training.
